There's a pervasive myth that "tax policy changes" are the reason economic inequality is growing. Actually it's because technology makes it increasingly easy to start companies, and makes them grow faster once you do.

https://t.co/ggPKu1hkY2

@mattyglesias If you go and look at specific instances of people who got really rich, you'll see that it isn't. Tax policies didn't make Larry and Sergey rich. Starting a tech company did. That's what has changed.

@ryangrim And that is exactly the sort of rich I analyzed in that essay — literally the 100 richest people in America. And the way they got rich was by starting companies, not from tax policy changes.

@ryangrim If you don't like people being so rich and you want to use tax policy to prevent it, that's a separate question from whether tax policy is how they got that way, so you should be clear about which of the two you're talking about.

@ryangrim While the answer to the second question is no (changes in tax policy are not what made Larry and Sergey rich), the answer to the first is obviously yes (changes in tax policy could prevent anyone from becoming or remaining rich). No one doubts that.

@ryangrim @Moreydoa @mustafajamil You're mistaken there. There's a point where high enough taxes make countries poorer. Sweden experimented with this in the 1970s, and pulled back after they hit the limit.

@LightCrownA @ryangrim @Moreydoa @mustafajamil You think you're asking clever rhetorical questions, but in fact they're answerable. Yes, we're close to the limit, because it's presumably where Sweden pulled back to, and the combined income tax rate in California (50.3%) is very close to Sweden's (52%).

